 article_revampLinks__mNkFw"><div><p>Synlait Milk said its fiscal 2026 year=
 was financially difficult but marked by a clear operational recovery, with=
 second-half performance improving sharply after a weak first half. The New=
 Zealand dairy processor reported total group revenue of NZD 1.93 billion, =
reported EBITDA of NZD 8.1 million and an underlying net loss after tax of =
NZD 21.6 million. The stock fell 6.35% to $0.30, leaving it near the bottom=
 of its 52-week range of $0.28 to $0.76.</p>
<p></p><h2><b>Key Takeaways</b></h2><p></p>
<p></p><ul><li>Synlait said FY 2026 was a year of heavy first-half losses f=
ollowed by a strong second-half rebound.</li><li>Reported net loss after ta=
x was NZD 75.4 million, while underlying net loss after tax was NZD 21.6 mi=
llion.</li><li>The North Island asset sale helped cut net debt by 14% to NZ=
D 215 million.</li><li>Food service delivered its first materially profitab=
le year, while consumer and food service both posted strong growth.</li><li=
>Management said it expects the second-half operating run rate to be sustai=
nable, but it is not giving formal earnings guidance yet.</li></ul><p></p>
<p></p><h2><b>Company Performance</b></h2><p></p>
<p>Synlait=E2=80=99s full-year results showed a business still under strain=
, but moving in the right direction. The company said first-half manufactur=
ing problems, weaker ingredients returns and deferred tax asset issues drov=
e a reported EBITDA loss of NZD 34.7 million in the first six months. The s=
econd half was much stronger, with reported EBITDA improving by NZD 77.5 mi=
llion and underlying EBITDA improving by NZD 38.1 million from the first ha=
lf.</p><div><div class=3D"flex flex-col gap-3 leading-normal [&amp;_img]:!m=
